From: Arjune <arjunevaibhavpatil1434@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dma-mapping: don't hand out highmem CMA pages from dma_common_alloc_pages

dma_common_alloc_pages() (used by dma_map_ops with .alloc_pages, e.g.
xen-swiotlb in a Xen dom0) clears the new page with
memset(page_address(page)), and dma_vmap_noncontiguous() returns
page_address() of it. A page from a CMA area in highmem has no kernel
address, so both get NULL.

On the Tinker Board RK3288 under Xen, dom0's CMA area is its second RAM
bank at 0x70000000, above lowmem, and the first USB webcam stream oopses
in uvc_alloc_urb_buffers() -> dma_alloc_noncontiguous() ->
dma_common_alloc_pages() (NULL pointer write in mmioset). Native Linux
uses dma-direct, which handles highmem, and is not affected.

Give a highmem CMA page back and allocate from lowmem instead.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/kernel/dma/ops_helpers.c	2026-10-27 16:41:21.261308893 +0530
+++ b/kernel/dma/ops_helpers.c	2026-10-27 16:41:21.306310949 +0530
@@ -65,6 +65,15 @@
 	struct page *page;
 
 	page = dma_alloc_contiguous(dev, size, gfp);
+	/*
+	 * The page is cleared (and used by dma_vmap_noncontiguous()) through
+	 * page_address(), which a highmem page doesn't have: take one from
+	 * lowmem instead when the CMA area is in highmem.
+	 */
+	if (page && PageHighMem(page)) {
+		dma_free_contiguous(dev, page, size);
+		page = NULL;
+	}
 	if (!page)
 		page = alloc_pages_node(dev_to_node(dev), gfp, get_order(size));
 	if (!page)
